[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "adr-2809041500-ayatori-carv2-pack-plane-and-disk-derived-suite"
  :adr/title "ayatori: CARv2 pack plane, IPNI tests, and a suite derived from disk"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-09-04"
  :adr/related ["ADR-2608160100" "ADR-2608021000" "ADR-2607300900"
                "adr-2809040800-kotobase-join-reach-is-composition-not-ref-count"
                "adr-2809041200-doubt-rules-not-grounded-in-properties"]
  :adr/context
  "kotoba-lang/ayatori calls itself 'the query plane woven from IPLD graphs and IPNI provider discovery'. Measured 2026-09-04 at pin cee2ebfd, three things were true of it at once.

FOUR ENTRY POINTS, FOUR GREENS. Every nbb entry reported 0 failures on a different suite: bin/run_tests.cljs 53 tests/106 assertions (GitHub CI), run-tests.cljs 66/137 (fleet gate, self-described as 'the whole suite'), run-tests-pure.cljs 18/47 (dependency-free gate), and all five namespaces together 77/165 (nothing ran this). The two fleet gates disagreed with each other: :jvm-test discovers namespaces through cognitect's runner and saw all five; nbb-cross-runtime-query runs run-tests.cljs and saw four, missing ayatori.remote-test -- the one namespace covering discovery -> fetch -> CID verification, which is what the repo is for. Both gates green, for weeks.

run-tests.cljs had already diagnosed the failure mode in a comment ('a runner that repeats the list can fall behind the suite and report a subset as a pass') and replaced its list with a t/run-all-tests namespace PATTERN. That does not fix it, for a reason the comment does not reach: the pattern filters namespaces that are LOADED, and what gets loaded is decided by the :require list the pattern was meant to replace. Its regex matches ayatori.remote-test; the require list omitted it. The guard was downstream of the thing it guarded.

THE IPNI PLANE HAD NO TESTS. ayatori.discovery had zero direct coverage while being half of what the repo is named for. Its async path's docstring claims 'Transport rejection is a failed router response, not a rejected aggregate Promise' -- a claim about a .catch that nothing verified, and whose absence turns one dead router into a lookup that never resolves while satisfying every other assertion.

CARV2 WAS ABSENT ENTIRELY. Zero references. The block path fetches one block per request, and the novelty chain it hydrates is a cons chain of width 1 (ADR-2608021000), so those round trips are strictly sequential and cannot be prefetched away.

THE DOCUMENTED REPRODUCTION NEVER WORKED. CI, README and the runner docstring all omitted org-nist-sha2, which io-multiformats requires as sha2.core, so every documented run died before the first test. Nobody noticed: Actions are disabled fleet-wide (ADR-2607300900), so the job never started."
  :adr/decision
  "1. THE SUITE IS DERIVED FROM DISK. ayatori.suite enumerates test/**/*_test.clj[cs] and runs exactly that set; the entry requires them at its top level (nbb awaits top-level loads and does not await one issued inside a function). There is no list to fall behind because there is no list. Two floors -- zero namespaces found, zero assertions executed -- each exit 2, which is neither a pass nor a failure. Shown both ways: adding a test file raises SCANNED 5 to 6 and the count to 78/167 with no edit anywhere; an empty tree exits 2 naming why. run-tests-pure.cljs stays a hand-written subset by design (a gate nobody can afford to run is not a gate) and now prints that it is one.

2. ayatori.discovery IS TESTED, with the transport injected. The content CID is never rewritten whatever a router answers; 404 and an empty provider list are answers rather than errors; a dead router is one failed response, not a failed lookup; quorum counts routers that answered, and all-routers-down (:all-routers-failed) is a different reason from :quorum-unmet. Discriminated: removing the .catch produces exactly one failure, in the test that names it; rewriting the cid fails the test that names that.

3. ayatori.pack READS CARv2 PACKS, AND REFUSES THE CONFIGURATION THAT LOOKS LIKE A SUCCESS. Per ADR-2608160100 a backend declares :block-per-object or :packed-blocks with no default, and :packed-blocks is refused unless the object plane also declares :range-read. That refusal cannot be deferred: without Range a packed reader still returns correct bytes by GETting the whole archive per block, so round trips go DOWN, every correctness assertion passes, and only transfer explodes. Nothing downstream can see it. The profile is checked before any request is issued, and that is asserted. Costs, in round trips rather than wall clock (this workstation runs many agents; a timing figure would describe the machine): 2 to open a pack (header, index) regardless of block count, N for N blocks, 0 for a block the pack does not carry (nil rather than a throw, so the caller falls back to the per-object path), 0 to re-read through the getter's memo. A pack with no index is refused. The codec stays in kotoba-lang/io-ipld-car -- this reads packs and never writes them, because a second encoder is how the :file-offset versus :payload-offset convention drifts. pack-fetcher is a drop-in fetch-fn, so provider-block-getter's rehash, provider fallback and memo are unchanged.

4. THE REPRODUCTION WORKS. org-nist-sha2 and io-ipld-car are in deps.edn, ci.yml, the README and the runner classpath. The documented command now produces the same counts as the local one.

Landed: kotoba-lang/ayatori#15 (072f9efa) and #16 (da2cee9f). West pin advanced cee2ebfd -> da2cee9f in two steps, each verified fast-forward and reachable from main."
  :adr/consequences
  "Measured at da2cee9f: nbb 91 tests / 239 assertions, JVM 87 / 231, both 0 failures, up from a CI that measured 53/106 and a fleet gate that measured 66/137. The nbb/JVM difference is #?(:cljs)-only tests and is NOT decomposed exactly -- a top-level (deftest count lands 6 short of what either runtime reports, so the arithmetic would be a guess. Both greens are what the gates read.

Two defects were found by writing the tests rather than by reasoning. read-block destructured :block from ipld.car.v2/read-frame, which returns :bytes, so it returned nil for every block -- and the round-trip test went GREEN, because ipld.car.bytes/equal? answers true for two nils and both sides were missing. Every byte comparison now goes through a some-bytes? floor. And open-pack first reassembled a fake archive to call read-index, which re-parses the header and reads at the file-absolute :index-offset, not where the index sits in a range response; it decodes the index bytes directly now.

A portability defect was found by running the other gate: the two new .cljc files used js/parseInt and unqualified clojure.string/ in code shared by both runtimes, which nbb resolves and clojure -M:test does not. Running only the runtime you are working in leaves the other gate red for a reason invisible from where you are standing.

The gates.edn table recording 'ayatori 35 / 62 ==' is annotated rather than rewritten: those numbers are provenance for why each gate was added, not a live claim, and nothing re-measures them. The == in particular was a measurement with a date that stopped being true without anything turning red.

Not done: no pack is written anywhere yet, so nothing in production reads through :packed-blocks -- the plane exists and is tested, and adopting it is a write-side decision (one commit, one pack) that has to be made where snapshots are committed. The round-trip counts above are measured against an in-memory range server, not against a real object store. The transfer-volume claim that justifies the :range-read refusal was argued from the format rather than measured -- SUPERSEDED 2026-09-04 by adr-2809041800, which measured it end to end and found it larger than the argument suggested: over-fetching tripled transfer (394,217 bytes against 109,764) while IMPROVING the round-trip count, which is why that refusal has to sit where both halves are visible. The same iteration removed the over-fetch itself (exact frame ranges derived from the index's neighbours), so the packed path now runs at 1.50x fewer round trips and parity transfer."}]
